Command Purpose <code>create</code> create a group, rect, image, shader or text node under a parent <code>attach</code> put a surface without an owner under a parent; the caller becomes its owner <code>update</code> change node properties. An update of only opacity, effects and meta of a node of another client answers with <code>node.requested</code> to its owner (D58), if the owner subscribes to <code>node.requested</code>; else it fails as other changes of a foreign node do, so the change is not lost unseen. An update that mixes other props with them fails with an error that names the other props. A confined client socket may send this request too. <code>animate</code> change properties over a duration with an easing curve <code>move</code> change the parent or the position among the siblings <code>remove</code> Remove a node. A removed surface becomes detached. With <code>to</code>, only the client <code>to</code> can attach the surface and gets <code>surface.detached</code>. <code>to</code> must have a connection (D55). <code>configure</code> send size, toplevel states (activated, tiled, maximized, fullscreen) and decoration mode to a client <code>focus</code> set the keyboard focus to an own surface, or clear it without a surface. A surface of another client answers with <code>node.requested</code> to its owner (D31). A client that cleared the focus may give it back once to the surface that had it, also of another client. A focus on another surface since then ends this return. <code>focus_node</code> give the keyboard to an own node that is no surface, or without a node give it back (see Keyboard focus of nodes) <code>close</code> ask a client to close a surface <code>camera</code> set the camera of an output that is or was plugged in, or restore its default camera without a transform; with a <code>duration</code>, move it there; the first client that sets the camera owns it, and a <code>camera</code> without a transform gives it up <p><code>animate</code> lets plugins and agents animate without code in every frame:</p> <pre><code>{"animate": {"id": "frame", "props": {"opacity": 0, "visible": false},
             "duration": 300, "easing": "ease_out"}}
</code></pre> <p>The core moves <code>transform</code>, <code>opacity</code>, <code>size</code> and <code>color</code> toward the targets along the curve: <code>linear</code> (the default), <code>ease_in</code>, <code>ease_out</code> or <code>ease_in_out</code>. The other properties change at the end, so this example fades the node out and then hides it.</p> <p><code>effects</code> in <code>animate</code> is the full list of the end. An effect of the list that has the same index and the same <code>shader</code> as the effect of the node now moves its numeric uniforms, a number or 2 to 4 numbers, toward the targets. All other changes, a new shader, a new uniform or a list of another length, come at the end. So a shader with a uniform <code>progress</code> runs from 0 to 1:</p> <pre><code>- update: {id: title, props: {effects: [{shader: /home/me/write.frag, uniforms: {progress: 0}}]}}
- animate: {id: title, props: {effects: [{shader: /home/me/write.frag, uniforms: {progress: 1}}]}, duration: 900}
</code></pre> <p>The duration is in milliseconds, at most one minute. A later <code>animate</code> or <code>update</code> of the node ends the animation where it is. The end sends <code>animation.done</code>.</p> <p>A camera moves the same way, with <code>duration</code> and <code>easing</code>. It starts where the camera is now, also in the middle of another move, and the end sends <code>animation.done</code> with the output and then <code>output.changed</code>:</p> <pre><code>{"camera": {"output": "DP-1", "transform": {"scale": 2, "translate": [-640, -400]},
            "duration": 500, "easing": "ease_in_out"}}
</code></pre>

Event Data <code>surface.created</code> a toplevel waits for a plugin: surface id, <code>app_id</code>, title, and <code>decoration</code>: <code>server</code> when the client asked for a frame of the plugins through <code>xdg-decoration</code>, <code>client</code> when it draws its own. <code>to</code> names the window manager that the core reserved it for (see Window managers). <code>pid</code> is the process of the client as the socket reports it, a hint that a pid namespace can hide; <code>token</code> is the activation token that mapped the surface, when one did. A token only reaches this event when the client asked for activation before its first commit. An activation in the same batch of requests counts too, as in foot. The core sends the event at the end of that batch. <code>surface.changed</code> surface id, <code>app_id</code>, title, size and <code>decoration</code>, after any of them changed <code>surface.destroyed</code>, <code>surface.detached</code> surface id. <code>surface.destroyed</code> follows only a <code>surface.created</code>. A toplevel that its client unmaps with a commit without a buffer also counts as destroyed. At its next initial commit, the core sends <code>surface.created</code> with the same id. After a <code>remove</code> with <code>to</code>, only <code>to</code> gets <code>surface.detached</code>. When <code>to</code> disconnects, the surface is free again, and all clients get <code>surface.detached</code>. With <code>resume</code>, this occurs at the end of the grace time. <code>focus.changed</code> the focused surface changed, by a <code>focus</code> of a client or by the core: <code>surface</code>, or <code>null</code>. Window managers draw their frames from it (D55). <code>surface.requested</code> surface id and <code>request</code>: <code>activate</code>, <code>close</code>, <code>fullscreen</code>, <code>unfullscreen</code>, <code>maximize</code>, <code>unmaximize</code> or <code>minimize</code>; a client or a taskbar asks, and plugins decide <code>node.requested</code> a client asks to change a node of another client: <code>node</code>, the asking <code>client</code>, <code>action</code> and <code>args</code>. The actions are <code>focus</code>, <code>move</code>, <code>resize</code>, <code>close</code> and <code>update</code>, with the arguments of the command; the arguments of <code>update</code> are its props. The core checks the props before it sends the request. Only the owner of the node gets it, and decides by doing the change or not (D31). An <code>update</code> of an owner that does not subscribe to <code>node.requested</code> fails with the error <code>belongs to</code>. Other changes of a foreign node stay refused. <code>input.key</code> the grabbed <code>binding</code> and its <code>state</code>: <code>pressed</code> each time its key goes down with its modifiers, <code>released</code> once when Ctrl, Alt or Super of the binding goes up (see IPC methods) <code>input.button</code>, <code>input.moved</code> the button of a grabbed button binding went down or up, or the pointer moved while it is down: <code>binding</code>, <code>state</code> (not for <code>input.moved</code>), <code>position</code> in scene coordinates, <code>output</code> and <code>local</code>, the position in the output; only to the connection of the grab <code>node.key</code> a key while a node of the client has the keyboard focus: node, keysym name, Linux code, state, modifiers and text (see Keyboard focus of nodes) <code>pointer.entered</code>, <code>pointer.left</code> the pointer came over or left the top node under it that takes input: node, and for <code>pointer.entered</code> <code>moved</code>: false when the scene moved under a pointer that stood still; not during a drag of a node or while locked <code>pointer.pressed</code> a button went down over a node that takes input: node, Linux button code, position in scene coordinates, or in output coordinates for a node under <code>screen:NAME</code>, and the <code>modifiers</code> that are down <code>pointer.moved</code>, <code>pointer.released</code> after a press over a rect or an image, until the button goes up: node, position and modifiers; <code>pointer.released</code> with the button <code>pointer.scroll</code> the wheel or the touchpad scrolled over a rect, an image or a shader node: node, position, <code>dx</code> and <code>dy</code> in logical pixels, <code>discrete</code> wheel steps, <code>source</code> (<code>wheel</code>, <code>finger</code>, <code>continuous</code> or <code>wheel_tilt</code>) and modifiers; only to the owner of the node <code>animation.done</code> the <code>node</code> whose animation reached its targets, or the <code>output</code> whose camera did <code>effect.failed</code> the shader of an effect failed after a change of its file; the single field <code>error</code> holds the path and the log, and the effect keeps its last working version <code>layers.dropped</code> the layers of these <code>nodes</code> did not fit into the pixel budget of a frame of the <code>output</code> and stay out of it; sent when the list changes, and an empty list means that all show again <code>effects.dropped</code> the watchdog removed the effects, the meshes and the shaders of shader nodes of the nodes of <code>client</code>, because frames were slow for two seconds and its visible nodes cost the most; if frames stay slow and that client's own load reaches the owner budget (D57), the core disconnects it and removes its nodes, also with <code>keep_nodes</code> or <code>resume</code>; below the budget the slowdown is shared, the effects of all clients go and none is disconnected <code>output.changed</code> output name, layout position and size, scale, camera, and the work area: the scene area that the output shows without panels and reserves; <code>default_work_area</code> is the same area as the default camera shows it, the place for windows that a camera move should not move <code>output.removed</code> output name <code>client.connected</code>, <code>client.disconnected</code> client name <code>method.called</code> another client calls a method that this connection exported: <code>call</code> id, <code>caller</code>, <code>method</code> and <code>params</code>; only the exporting connection gets it, subscribed or not <code>NAME.something</code> a client emitted it with <code>events.emit</code>, for example <code>voice.state</code>: <code>client</code>, the name of the emitter, and its <code>data</code>; only to the connections that name the event or a pattern of it (D65) <p>Every event carries <code>seq</code>, the count of its connection (D32). Every event that the core pushes to a connection carries the next number of that connection, also before <code>events.subscribe</code>. The answer of <code>events.subscribe</code> is a snapshot of the current scene, the same list that <code>scene.query</code> gives for all nodes. The snapshot covers the state before it, the events that follow cover every change after it. A number that skips tells the client that it missed an event, so it queries again. After a reconnect a client works from the new snapshot and forgets the old numbers.</p> <p>A client emits events of its own with <code>events.emit</code> (D65), for example a voice agent that tells a HUD its state:</p> <pre><code>{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 7, "method": "events.emit",
 "params": {"event": "voice.state", "data": {"state": "listening"}}}
{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "method": "event",
 "params": {"event": "voice.state", "client": "voice", "data": {"state": "listening"}, "seq": 12}}
</code></pre> <ul> <li>The name starts with the client name and a dot. The rest has lowercase   letters, digits, <code>_</code>, <code>-</code> and <code>.</code>, from a letter, at most 64 bytes. So   no client sends a core event or the event of another name. A client   whose name starts as a core namespace, such as <code>surface</code> or <code>output</code>,   cannot emit.</li> <li>A connection subscribes to the name, for example <code>voice.state</code>, or to   a pattern <code>PREFIX.*</code>, for example <code>voice.*</code>. A pattern also works for   core events, for example <code>surface.*</code>. An empty list means every core   event, not the events of clients. In <code>events.subscribe</code>, a name outside   the core namespaces is the event of a client, so a typo there is no   error; a typo in a core name, such as <code>surface.creatd</code>, is.</li> <li><code>data</code> is any JSON value of at most 64 KiB; <code>null</code> when it is missing.</li> <li>A client name emits at most 32 events per second, over all its   connections. The core refuses the rest with code 6 and sends nothing.   It does not coalesce: after a burst, the client sends its last state   again.</li> <li>Any named client may emit, also through a client socket without   capabilities: an event reaches only subscribers. Any connection may   subscribe to the events of other clients, also a client socket; it   gets them as it gets core events. The emitter gets its own event when   it subscribed to it.</li> <li>Only the main socket lets a client take a free name. A name of a client   socket is safe from emitters on other sockets (D33).</li> </ul> <p>The response of <code>scene.apply</code> reports the result of a transaction. When the transaction applied, the result is <code>{"warnings": [...]}</code>. When it did not apply, the response is an error with the index of the failed command. A warning does not stop the transaction. It tells the client that a command applied but does not give the result that the client probably expects (D59). Each warning has a stable <code>code</code>, the <code>node</code> and a short <code>message</code>:</p> <pre><code>{"warnings": [{"code": "deaf_surface", "node": "s1",
               "message": "an ancestor has input pass or block, so the surface gets no pointer input"}]}
</code></pre> Code Cause <code>deaf_surface</code> an <code>attach</code> or a <code>move</code> puts a surface under an ancestor with <code>input: pass</code> or <code>block</code>; the surface gets no pointer input <code>layer_over_budget</code> after a <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>animate</code>, <code>attach</code> or <code>move</code>, the layer textures of a layer node and its descendants need more pixels than the budget of one frame on every output; the core counts them at the smallest scale of the outputs. The node is the named node or its outermost layer ancestor that passes the budget, so a child added under a big layer warns for that layer. No frame draws the node <p>The pixel budget of a frame is 64 Mi pixels. The layers that do not fit into a frame because of other layers are no warning: the event <code>layers.dropped</code> tells them when the frame shows.</p> <p><code>choractl apply FILE</code> reads one list of commands as JSON, from standard input when the file is <code>-</code>. It prints nothing when it applied and the reason when not, so the exit code is the answer. It prints each warning on standard error and exits with success. Only the <code>view</code> subcommand reads YAML. A request that a client makes before its <code>surface.created</code> follows that event. A taskbar asks through <code>wlr-foreign-toplevel-management</code>. The core does not act on a request, also not on <code>close</code>. A plugin answers with commands, for example <code>close</code>. A press over a surface reaches its client as well; a press over a rect or an image goes to plugins only, with the moves and the release that follow, so that a plugin can drag a window by its title bar. <code>modifiers</code> lists <code>Ctrl</code>, <code>Alt</code>, <code>Shift</code> and <code>Super</code> as they are down, for example for a Ctrl+click.</p> <p>Scrolling over a surface reaches its client through Wayland. Over a rect, an image or a shader node, the core sends <code>pointer.scroll</code> to the connections of the owner of the node that subscribed to it, so that a list of a plugin scrolls:</p> <pre><code>{"event": "pointer.scroll", "node": "start-menu", "position": [40, 700],
 "dx": 0, "dy": 15, "discrete": [0, 1], "source": "wheel", "modifiers": []}
</code></pre> <p>The number of nodes has no limit. The watchdog limits their cost: when frames stay slow, the effects of the client whose nodes cost the most go (see <code>effects.dropped</code>). The core disconnects that client only when its own load reaches the owner budget (D57). <code>CHORA_OWNER_LOAD_BUDGET</code> sets that budget, 4000 by default, as <code>Scene::load_by_owner</code> counts. When no client alone reaches it, the slowdown is shared: all effects go and no client goes. Later milestones add events for size limits.</p>
